window.DIGEST.following.push(...[{"id": "2112807106780762563", "url": "https://x.com/kimmonismus/status/2112807106780762563", "text": "Dario Amodei says Anthropic held back broad access to Mythos-class models for four months to strengthen cyber safeguards. Mythos was originally scheduled for release in February this year. \n\nHe also says distillation poses no major commercial threat, but raises security concerns, and Anthropic has blocked suspected attempts to use its models for bioweapons.", "name": "Chubby♨️", "handle": "kimmonismus", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1728327996375719936/RW7VBJfD_200x200.jpg", "likes": 781, "rts": 35, "replies": 79, "views": 64759, "ts": 1792567442, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVIyrgoWIAAVZ_0.jpg?name=orig"}, {"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVIyyPbXAAAJHgh.jpg?name=orig"}, {"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVIy3MJXMAA1nfU.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": {"id": "2112732230124818891", "url": "https://x.com/DarioAmodei/status/2112732230124818891", "text": "Thank you for this, @_NathanCalvin. I appreciate and am a fan of the investigative work you’ve done on behalf of AI safety and believe your comments are coming from a constructive, honest place, and that it benefits the public to have people whose job is to scrutinize the actions of frontier AI companies. I think something would be wrong if companies of the size of Anthropic didn’t get harshly criticized basically every day; strong antibodies against corporate power are a healthy feature of a free society, and especially so when issues of the gravity of the one we’re facing are at play. I also do agree that some of the examples you cite were mistakes by Anthropic.\n\nThat said, I disagree with the thrust of your post. At the core, a good fraction of the things you criticize are consequences of Anthropic’s basic theory of change that we have held for many years. Our strategy has always been what I’ve called the “race to the top” — by showing how it’s possible to build the technology responsibly, we can raise the bar for the entire industry. The forces that drive the race to the top are hugely more effective if the company practicing them is at the frontier; that’s one lesson I’ve learned repeatedly. And looking at that strategy as a whole, I’m proud of our track record and stand by it.\n\nI believe that if we take a list of things that Anthropic did in the past year that were pro-safety and commercially costly to us, they far outweigh the list you’ve made, while at the same time I also disagree with some of the items on your list. Here are some things Anthropic has done, in the same period you’ve cited (the last year), which were very costly to us commercially while having strong safety benefits:\n\n1. We played a key role in SB53 (and other state laws), at a time when doing so was politically perilous to the company and AI regulation was very unpopular. We in fact argued for a stronger version of SB53 than was ultimately passed; there were a lot of stakeholders and we were not the limiting factor in the strength of the bill that was passed.\n\n2. We have very strong biological safeguards on our models, because we’ve seen concerning results in bio uplift trials and even seen and blocked potential attempts to misuse our models for bioweapons (as seen in our threat intelligence report from a few weeks ago). This is a very serious threat that we want to have zero tolerance for. But having very low false negatives means having significant false positives (despite months of effort on our part to improve the classifiers), enough so that biologists joke about how restrictive our classifiers are, and competitors sometimes sell against us by touting their more permissive bio capabilities. This has been very painful to us in an area where we care a lot about supporting an ecosystem of Claude users (biomedical science; and it is particularly frustrating to me as a biologist), but it’s the right thing to do and we intend to keep doing it.\n\n3. Similarly, we first had Mythos-class models in February but didn’t make them generally available until June (when we had stronger cyber safeguards in place). We only offered them to a limited set of partners in order to focus on cyberdefense. It would have been much more economically rational to simply release the model to everyone. We likely sacrificed a significant chunk of a several-month model lead. But because of this, many companies secured their cyber infrastructure before the deluge of widely available cyber-capable models. Of course there are ways it could have gone better or been run better, but this was what we were aiming to do.\n\n4. I advocated forcefully and publicly, in my essay Policy on the AI Exponential in June of this year, for the government to have the power to block model releases. Back then this was still outside or maybe on the edge of the Overton window, though I understand the window has moved since then. We were attacked intensely for this.\n\n5. I don’t want to re-ignite conflict, but the events of February 2026 are a strong example. It is clear that our public stance could have seriously damaged the company’s future.\n\n6. We’ve continued to advocate for export controls on chips and chipmaking equipment to China, because we think it allows democracies to negotiate from a position of strength on AI safety agreements. We’ve done this despite problems it’s created for us with industry partners who hold very different views and on whom we are very dependent.\n\n7. As far as I’m aware we were the first, and still only, frontier company to restrict the sale of AI services to PRC-controlled companies, including those operating through third countries (otherwise a major loophole). We’ve therefore forfeited significant short-term business to prevent fueling AI platforms and applications that would help the CCP’s military and intelligence services. We are also cracking down on distillation; generally, distillation is not a major threat to us commercially, but like chip exports it has concerning national security and safety implications, so we expend significant energy and company resources to address it.\n\n8. As you mentioned, we’ve put out a lot of reports, announcements, long model cards, public advocacy, etc, all trying to inform the public about the risks of AI. These involve a level of detail and effort that would be implausible for a purely profit-maximizing company.\n\n9. Just this week, we announced we were putting 5% (and possibly more over time) of the company into a public benefit fund.\n\nAll of these decisions came with very substantial costs to us. To be fair, those that involved holding back or restricting access to frontier capabilities also had a broader cost — for example, the public might otherwise have benefitted from faster progress in biology, so I am not saying that our decisions were without tradeoffs — but they were not driven by profit-seeking.\n\nFor each decision, you can come up with ulterior motives that could have driven us — I think it is a general truth that any decision, on any topic, can always be portrayed as a nefarious scheme. But most of those end up being pretty farfetched or don’t fully make sense if you actually examine them closely. For example, it is deeply implausible that it would be a shrewd business move to block a large swath of research biology in order to do the work ourselves and compete with our customers; we expect the benefit to the ecosystem to greatly exceed the work Anthropic does itself, and for the two to complement each other. It is not in Anthropic’s interest to be perceived as unfriendly to biologists. Our Life Sciences Verification Program, which gives vetted biology professionals access to our models, also refutes this idea. \n\nOf course, a different set of people will see this and make a different criticism: that Anthropic shouldn't be making decisions about who gets access to what in the first place. I agree, in the sense that ideally the government would be making these decisions, and that’s why we advocate for regulation. But for now we are forced to make them, and enabling bioterrorism isn’t a better option.\n\nI think that even if I conceded every item in your list, I would happily hold up mine next to it; I don’t think it’s a close call. That is of course partly subjective and different things are important to different people. Maybe one way you could object is that several of the items above relate to AI misuse and not loss-of-control; if you think the latter is much more important than the former, that might change your calculus. But this is not Anthropic’s view — we view both as serious issues. You can also disagree with our decisions, as many have, but they are consistent.\n\nBut I also disagree with at least some of your list. Several items don’t represent a deviation from Anthropic’s strategy or values at all, once we postulate the “race to the top” strategy that requires being a frontier AI company. Your items 1-4 are all simply examples of what it means to compete at the frontier, and we stand by them, both in the sense that they are transparently ordinary things to do as part of that strategy, and that we stand by the strategy as a whole. \n\nTo be fair, there are important questions worth confronting (and that we are confronting) about what to do when the AI-driven acceleration gets fast enough that it is hard for safety to keep up. We are nearing that point and that’s what’s driving some of our thinking about “pacing.” But 1-4 are actions taken before that point and should be perfectly expected based on Anthropic’s strategy as a company. Of course it is possible to disagree with that strategy, but that’s a more fundamental disagreement that you could have had any time over the last few years.\n\nI agree we could have handled your items 7, 8, and 9 better. All fall into the category of “rhetoric or policy communication that wasn’t carefully written or resulting from fast-changing circumstances not propagating from one team to another fast enough.” This kind of thing happens a lot in large organizations (Anthropic is over 5,000 people), and doesn’t even necessarily represent a failure or mistake of any single person. I am of course the CEO, and I take responsibility. The volume of communication from Anthropic is enormous and if these are the worst examples, I think we’re frankly doing pretty well, though I will continue to work hard to make sure we do even better.\n\nYour 5th item (the change to our RSP) is the most substantive item, and people can debate this, but I think that the old, more rigid RSP created a lot of situations where we were technically required to do something that required a lot of effort but in retrospect wasn’t very important for safety, as well as not covering some of the most important things. Our current RSP’s focus on transparency brought things like risk reports and roadmaps, and its emphasis on independent external review laid the groundwork for even more intensive embedded evaluation, all of which I think have been valuable in driving a race to the top. In the original announcement in September 2023, we described the RSP as an “early iteration that we [would] build on” and said that “rapid iteration…will almost certainly be necessary.” I understand that changing plans is always a move that creates justifiable scrutiny — I just don’t think we ever represented that we wouldn’t do so.\n\nRelating to your 6th item (having an FCF in addition to our RSP), I know our intent is to meet the letter and spirit of our regulatory obligations (via our FCF) while also remaining able to experiment with more novel approaches to risk management that go beyond what the law currently requires (via our RSP). I promise to look into it further, as it’s a complex issue and it is possible we are not weighing the considerations in the right way.\n\nOverall, although Anthropic has made plenty of mistakes as an organization — including some of the items on your list — I feel that we have repeatedly taken serious and substantive actions counter to our bottom line and that there is a significant negativity bias distorting assessments like the one you are making above. We’ll always try to do better but, again, I am extremely proud of our track record and stand by it. I will have more to say soon about how we are going to further live our values in this moment of rapid change.", "name": "Dario Amodei", "handle": "DarioAmodei", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2015835742577012736/uOwdzrEz_200x200.jpg", "likes": 3925, "rts": 302, "replies": 703, "views": 1350149, "ts": 1792549590, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.26, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112986457040134166", "url": "https://x.com/Google/status/2112986457040134166", "text": "From drawing in webcam “fog\" to playing HTML synths, the builds with Gemini 4 Argon are incredibly creative.\n\nHere’s what developers are spinning up 🧵↓", "name": "Google", "handle": "Google", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2042749771337564160/AgOFPEL3_200x200.jpg", "likes": 404, "rts": 33, "replies": 34, "views": 665546, "ts": 1792610203, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.24, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112993956611178996", "url": "https://x.com/felixrieseberg/status/2112993956611178996", "text": "If you're into research previews, we're opening up a waitlist for Claude Duet, our take on a lightweight proactive agent.\n\nThe form factor will seem pretty familiar, lots of agents out there - but Duet is made for work, excellent judgement, and making sure it doesn't do things you didn't want. \n\nhttps://claude.com/product/claude-duet", "name": "Felix Rieseberg", "handle": "felixrieseberg", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1544558915819487233/qMrauBqx_200x200.jpg", "likes": 411, "rts": 12, "replies": 55, "views": 20054, "ts": 1792611991, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112993433606615040/vid/avc1/1280x720/Jd2YxrB1vnSQFRue.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112993433606615040/vid/avc1/1920x1080/gzWlVAzk12KVKHJn.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112993433606615040/img/MtgXzWyHbxNDjema.jpg", "dur": 99}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.24, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112899856205578486", "url": "https://x.com/OpenAI/status/2112899856205578486", "text": "In the same mouse-brain dataset, GPT-Rosalind found a possible clue to why TSC2 mutations tend to cause more severe tuberous sclerosis than TSC1 mutations.\n\nThe two genes work together to regulate mTOR. Yet in one group of cerebellar neurons, disrupting TSC2 changed the expression of dozens of genes, including genes involved in neuronal communication. Disrupting TSC1 had little effect on gene expression in the same cell type.\n\nExisting treatments already target mTOR. Do they reverse these changes, or is there more to TSC2’s role in these neurons?", "name": "OpenAI", "handle": "OpenAI", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1885410181409820672/ztsaR0JW_200x200.jpg", "likes": 614, "rts": 28, "replies": 21, "views": 100838, "ts": 1792589556, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2112899854569762936", "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.24, "thread": [{"id": "2112899854569762936", "url": "https://x.com/OpenAI/status/2112899854569762936", "text": "GPT-Rosalind surfaced a new lead for drug discovery: could restoring cholesterol transport protect neurons from degeneration?\n\nDCTN1 is a gene linked to Perry syndrome, a rare neurodegenerative disorder. In mouse-brain data, disrupting it increased expression of genes involved in making cholesterol. GPT-Rosalind suggested a possible explanation: cholesterol may not be reaching where neurons need it.\n\nThat gives scientists a specific mechanism to test and, if confirmed, a direction for developing new treatments for neurodegenerative disorders.\n\nWith @perturbai_tx and @scrippsresearch, a team of thousands of GPT-Rosalind agents worked in parallel, connecting gene-expression results from millions of mouse brain cells with scientific literature in just 32 minutes. We’re now expanding this collaboration to identify new drug targets.\n\nhttps://openai.com/index/scaling-ai-research-for-complex-biological-data/", "name": "OpenAI", "handle": "OpenAI", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1885410181409820672/ztsaR0JW_200x200.jpg", "likes": 6098, "rts": 626, "replies": 354, "views": 1121758, "ts": 1792589555, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112895264465772735", "url": "https://x.com/Dr_Singularity/status/2112895264465772735", "text": "Billions of humanoid robots.\n\nRobots will be capable of essentially all economically important tasks by 2029.\n\nThe age of human labor is ending. The age of robotic abundance is beginning.\n\nThe economic growth we'll see as the humanoid robot workforce expands will be unlike anything in history.\n\nASI + humanoids. We could see global GDP doubling in just a few years and soon after that, every 12 months.\n\nYou will see a global economy 1,000 times bigger in your lifetime.", "name": "Dr Singularity", "handle": "Dr_Singularity", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1607111366397120514/j4XzXC27_200x200.jpg", "likes": 504, "rts": 63, "replies": 30, "views": 17292, "ts": 1792588461, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112892705600618649", "url": "https://x.com/bercankilic/status/2112892705600618649", "text": "Sparks of abundance by 2029 https://x.com/i/article/2112886023566868480", "name": "Bercan", "handle": "bercankilic", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2076603421260419072/nMCg2Bl5_200x200.jpg", "likes": 241, "rts": 33, "replies": 42, "views": 37291, "ts": 1792587851, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.24, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112922030165090573", "url": "https://x.com/TZahavy/status/2112922030165090573", "text": "Over the past year, the Discovery team at @GoogleDeepMind, in a project led by student researcher @dsantosmarco, has made new discoveries in one of the most beautiful open problems at the edge of computation: Finding the 6th Busy Beaver (BB) number.\n\nWhile most AI math systems solve human-generated problems, like theorems, this work tackles a different aspect of mathematical creativity: Discovering hidden problems.\n\nFor years, the BB Community has pursued one of theoretical computer science’s most captivating collaborative quests: finding the simplest programs that run the longest before halting. Because determining whether these programs ever stop is uncomputable, no universal algorithm can solve it, meaning new mathematical techniques need to be found and invented to make progress.\n\nIn this way, the BB challenge serves as an open ended domain for mathematical creativity and, in particular, a generator of hard math problems called Cryptids. These are tiny programs whose halting behavior is governed by and equivalent to deep, unsolved math problems in number theory, similar to the Collatz or Goldbach conjectures. Historically, the BB community had recognized ~20 Cryptids in BB(6), and the best problems were selected for the Busy Math Olympiad (BMO).\n\nBuilding on their work, Marco built a pipeline powered by Gemini 4 Argon that:\n1️⃣ Simulates raw Turing machine traces to compress recurring patterns.\n2️⃣ Conjectures mathematical transition rules & runs synthetic experiments to test edge cases.\n3️⃣ Proves the equivalence formally in Lean 4.  Using this pipeline, Marco and the team discovered 20 new potential Cryptids and verified 33 more machines previously studied in the BB community. All the proofs were open sourced and shared in the community discord channel and we hope that they will help the community to make progress towards resolving BB(6). We also contributed related halting/non-halting proofs directly to the community GitHub repository.\n\nFinding these Cryptids shows hidden links between simple programs and number theory, and helps mathematicians map the limits of computation. I'm also excited about Gemini 4 Argon's ability to help researchers both solve hard problems and accelerate scientific discoveries, of which Busy Beaver is just one example.\n\nWork led by our student researcher @dsantosmarco with paper contributions from @vivek_veeriah, @Dahoas1 , Andrew Bolt, @zuza777 , Satinder Singh, and myself with additional contributions from Hussain Masoom, @maxencefaldor , @MoritzFirsching and Petar Sirkovic.\n\nRead more about it in our paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22603", "name": "Tom Zahavy", "handle": "TZahavy", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1984383190815952896/DGQA3E0j_200x200.jpg", "likes": 422, "rts": 68, "replies": 24, "views": 36687, "ts": 1792594842, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKbYfcW8AA_dS-.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.23, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112898254811926727", "url": "https://x.com/haider1/status/2112898254811926727", "text": "demis hassabis will be expecting AGI sooner than even his own recent predictions\n\njan 2025: AGI is still 5-10 years away and possibly one or two breakthroughs are needed\n\nfeb 2026: we're at another threshold moment where AGI is on the horizon, possibly within the next 5 years\n\noct 2026: now expects AGI within 2-3 years", "name": "Haider.", "handle": "haider1", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2047286007003848704/u2mzg86I_200x200.jpg", "likes": 532, "rts": 46, "replies": 27, "views": 19769, "ts": 1792589174, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKCUboaEAEIdGZ.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.22, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112947752527139134", "url": "https://x.com/robertwiblin/status/2112947752527139134", "text": "My plea to all AI lab staff: reassess if your current project is your best opportunity to delay RSI, or stop it blowing up in our face.\n\nIf it's not, move immediately.\n\nThere's no time to waste even a week to inertia or politeness.\n\nThe strategic situation is shifting under our feet, so if you haven't reassessed your options recently, you could easily be wasting your time or possibly making things worse.\n\nYou have access to info I don't, but here's 8 ways priorities seem to have shifted to me, which I elaborate on in the video:\n\n1. Helping with capabilities advances looks worse than in the past, especially bringing forward RSI\n\n2. Safety & alignment evals need a credibility revolution to stop misleading the public\n\n3. 'Pacing the frontier' is unambiguously necessary, and working to make it happen is a huge new opportunity for talent\n\n4. Chain-of-thought monitoring probably stops working without a big technical and regulatory push to keep it\n\n5. It's worse than ever to patch surface indications of deep structural problems you know aren't getting fixed\n\n6. There's ever less need for altruists to help with primarily commercial work\n\n7. Staff have an ever-shrinking window to organise as a group and collectively shape events\n\n8. There are new opportunities for research impact outside frontier companies that are worth considering\n\nI know you're probably some combination of exhausted, overwhelmed, and anxious already.\n\nBut unfortunately there's no way to escape the need to regularly check in on the high level strategic picture and see where you can slot in to maximum effect.\n\nI wish I could do it for you but nobody far from your situation can. Godspeed my friends.\n\n(From the 80,000 Hours Podcast, links below.)", "name": "Rob Wiblin", "handle": "robertwiblin", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1890364551423504384/VSUxxPid_200x200.jpg", "likes": 364, "rts": 60, "replies": 18, "views": 15084, "ts": 1792600975, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112943728566378498/vid/avc1/1280x720/rKvkIrgbSU5cKnrm.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112943728566378498/vid/avc1/1920x1080/Iiah07VNkDUTB1dT.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112943728566378498/img/3BJkiKTQd-exkH73.jpg", "dur": 866}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Video, Art & Music", "score": 6.2, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112952051911262545", "url": "https://x.com/alibaba_cloud/status/2112952051911262545", "text": "Alibaba Cloud is partnering with @Theta_Network EdgeCloud to bring Qwen AI models to Web3-native developers worldwide. \n\nThis milestone integration extends our frontier AI models, including Qwen3.8-Max and Qwen3.8-Flash, to a decentralized GPU compute network. We are empowering developers with greater flexibility, distributed infrastructure, and direct access to enterprise-grade AI.", "name": "Alibaba Cloud", "handle": "alibaba_cloud", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2049406152564461568/0-ZrbCS9_200x200.jpg", "likes": 259, "rts": 71, "replies": 25, "views": 156695, "ts": 1792602000, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HU9RKggbkAAjqQF.png?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.16, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112981561591107979", "url": "https://x.com/testingcatalog/status/2112981561591107979", "text": "OpenAI ❤️ Google Cloud\n\nGPT-6 Astra, GPT-6.1 Sol, and GPT-6 Luna models are now available on Google Cloud. \n\n> \"Google Cloud is now the only hyperscale cloud provider offering access to all three leading closed-source LLMs\" (aka, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google). \n\nGoooood 😈", "name": "🚨 AI News | TestingCatalog", "handle": "testingcatalog", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1899962463115751424/i-6MBWau_200x200.jpg", "likes": 390, "rts": 17, "replies": 21, "views": 15191, "ts": 1792609036, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLRN8EW0AAS61a.jpg?name=orig"}, {"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLRP9XXMAA9jN1.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": {"id": "2112896659608785181", "url": "https://x.com/googlecloud/status/2112896659608785181", "text": "Today, we're bringing OpenAI models to Google Cloud's model garden.\n\nWe're now the only hyperscale cloud provider offering access to all three leading closed source LLMs—alongside 200+ other models—on a unified enterprise platform → https://goo.gle/4zJ0VZQ", "name": "Google Cloud", "handle": "googlecloud", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2047008530478346240/2ZoDIjwe_200x200.jpg", "likes": 930, "rts": 148, "replies": 92, "views": 102061, "ts": 1792588794, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKEUKPWEAA9mvu.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.16, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112799464792482026", "url": "https://x.com/kimmonismus/status/2112799464792482026", "text": "Now *thats* some big upgrade: Tibo says logged-in Codex and Work users now get *permanent prompt caching.* That’s a big upgrade for long coding sessions.\n\nYou can leave a large project conversation, come back hours or days later, and reuse the cached context without losing the benefit simply because you took a break.", "name": "Chubby♨️", "handle": "kimmonismus", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1728327996375719936/RW7VBJfD_200x200.jpg", "likes": 628, "rts": 22, "replies": 75, "views": 32911, "ts": 1792565620, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112712984644784534", "url": "https://x.com/thsottiaux/status/2112712984644784534", "text": "Day 16/\n\nYou can now cache for 12h in the API and for logged in users across Codex & Work we keep cache forever and you don’t have any penalty for coming back to an old conversation or resuming after a few hours and are now guaranteed to hit the cache.", "name": "Tibo", "handle": "thsottiaux", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2093807917833281537/2yBgpwVV_200x200.jpg", "likes": 15189, "rts": 677, "replies": 2560, "views": 2660414, "ts": 1792545002, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.15, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112997065668346323", "url": "https://x.com/AndrewCurran_/status/2112997065668346323", "text": "Funny how these days once things start to tip - in this case proactive agents - it turns into an avalanche. I posted about Grok sending me a message proactively a few days ago. This is great news for people like me who have attention issues with exactly this type of thing.", "name": "Andrew Curran", "handle": "AndrewCurran_", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1596945208058744833/_X3LT7fb_200x200.jpg", "likes": 362, "rts": 13, "replies": 24, "views": 16155, "ts": 1792612732, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLfoeiaYAEObiu.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": {"id": "2112984664256241837", "url": "https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112984664256241837", "text": "We’re opening a research preview of Claude Duet, a new app where Claude works as a proactive agent.\n\nIt comes to you with what needs your attention, completes tasks you ask of it, keeps working when you step away, and checks with you before it acts on your behalf.", "name": "Claude", "handle": "claudeai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1950950107937185792/QOfEjFoJ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 43204, "rts": 3271, "replies": 1714, "views": 5525351, "ts": 1792609775, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112982862186106880/vid/avc1/640x360/L7zosiEUW76tqYr-.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112982862186106880/vid/avc1/640x360/L7zosiEUW76tqYr-.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLUnMDWsAAn7Pw.png", "dur": 100}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.15, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112957740326989956", "url": "https://x.com/ollama/status/2112957740326989956", "text": "Meet Flint from @Recursive_SI! A ~40B parameter MoE open model for fast, accurate agentic coding use case made to run on-device. \n\nFlint can generate >125 tps on a M4 Pro Macbook Pro and >150 tps on a 64GB NVIDIA RTX Spark on short contexts. \n\nTry it on Ollama today: \nollama run flint\n\nClaude Code: \nollama launch claude --model flint", "name": "ollama", "handle": "ollama", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2045273960666071040/rCjCIwiC_200x200.jpg", "likes": 327, "rts": 26, "replies": 36, "views": 24169, "ts": 1792603356, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112944385415426249", "url": "https://x.com/RichardSocher/status/2112944385415426249", "text": "Introducing Flint, @Recursive_SI’s first model: a fast local coding model built with our automated research system, the Eureka Machine.\n\n• Best-in-class fast local coding\n• Up to 2x faster than comparable models: 125+ tokens/sec on a MacBook Pro, 150+ on RTX Spark\n• Built from scratch in under 4 months\n\nPrivate, unmetered intelligence on your own machine. Open weights available now on @huggingface, run it locally on your favorite framework or try it for free on @opencode or @pidotdev.", "name": "Richard Socher", "handle": "RichardSocher", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1232361282759610368/YYxndVmI_200x200.jpg", "likes": 6093, "rts": 643, "replies": 485, "views": 2497893, "ts": 1792600172, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112941750809419776/vid/avc1/1280x720/ljeVLZeytT--ckEJ.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112941750809419776/vid/avc1/3840x2160/OjycwsU9wNo0PMbi.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112941750809419776/img/MJaHRrNTo-Kg92Pb.jpg", "dur": 161}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.13, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112919623276372441", "url": "https://x.com/Dr_Singularity/status/2112919623276372441", "text": "this is amazing \n\n1000s of AI agents compress months of biological analysis into just 32 minutes\n\nOpenAI, PerturbAI and Scripps Research deployed 1000s of GPT‑Rosalind agents to analyze data from millions of mouse brain cells in 32 minutes, work researchers estimate would take humans months to a year. \n\nThey uncovered promising leads related to brain disease, including a potential link between cholesterol regulation and neurodegeneration. \n\n“We build cell atlases so that other scientists can make discoveries we haven’t imagined yet. AI could help more researchers explore these resources and turn unexpected observations into new questions to test.”\n\nThese are testable hypotheses awaiting experiments, but the speed is extraordinary.", "name": "Dr Singularity", "handle": "Dr_Singularity", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1607111366397120514/j4XzXC27_200x200.jpg", "likes": 365, "rts": 48, "replies": 17, "views": 9808, "ts": 1792594268, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKY73uW8AA-0ac.png?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.13, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112911688349716665", "url": "https://x.com/MTSlive/status/2112911688349716665", "text": "SITUATION DETECTED: Critical PHP vulnerabilities were discovered with the help of GPT-5.6 Sol. OpenAI says these are among several significant vulnerabilities they have found and are working to fix, and they expect similar findings to \"increase sharply\" in the coming months.", "name": "MTS", "handle": "MTSlive", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2057947669281263616/aovpVL4b_200x200.jpg", "likes": 443, "rts": 32, "replies": 2, "views": 16914, "ts": 1792592377, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.12, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112961511895289886", "url": "https://x.com/charliermarsh/status/2112961511895289886", "text": "An update here: we’re upgrading all Codex for OSS grants to Pro 200!\n\nThere will still be the 10,000 grants in this round, now at the higher tier. (If you've already received a grant, the upgrade should be reflected automatically.)\n\nApplications are still open :)", "name": "Charlie Marsh", "handle": "charliermarsh", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1490182599981125634/ex0Jezvf_200x200.jpg", "likes": 326, "rts": 13, "replies": 45, "views": 56052, "ts": 1792604256, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2099965140233847246", "url": "https://x.com/charliermarsh/status/2099965140233847246", "text": "We started Codex for OSS six months ago.\n\nToday, we’re renewing the program with $100 Pro plans and doubling the number of grants (from 5,000 to 10,000) to reach more maintainers.\n\n(If you received a grant previously, we encourage you to re-apply!)\n\nhttps://openai.com/form/codex-for-oss/", "name": "Charlie Marsh", "handle": "charliermarsh", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1490182599981125634/ex0Jezvf_200x200.jpg", "likes": 2574, "rts": 175, "replies": 199, "views": 916046, "ts": 1789505679, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.11, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2113004898929299492", "url": "https://x.com/testingcatalog/status/2113004898929299492", "text": "ANTHROPIC 🔥: An early look at how agentic shopping flow with Stripe Link would work on Claude Duet, Anthropic's upcoming proactive agent. \n\nInteresting to see these hashtags under the agent - does it mean that we will see its UI designed around \"threaded\" conversations like on Slack? \n\nFun fact: Duet's internal name is \"aibo\".\n\nAPKPLZ 👀 https://x.com/stripe/status/2112995753824301406/video/1", "name": "🚨 AI News | TestingCatalog", "handle": "testingcatalog", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1899962463115751424/i-6MBWau_200x200.jpg", "likes": 304, "rts": 12, "replies": 25, "views": 16177, "ts": 1792614600, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112995559674146816/vid/avc1/960x720/RZb2LrJcdxcDtMNZ.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112995559674146816/vid/avc1/2880x2160/Rbf9OPuxT5dVocEX.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLgk6UaYAEOuf_.jpg", "dur": 48}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.1, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112808451353047427", "url": "https://x.com/petergostev/status/2112808451353047427", "text": "In Codex you cannot miss cache if you let it expire. \n\nBefore if you had full 475k context and you miss cache, it would cost you $9.5 API equivalent to send any message, now it would cost you $0.95.\n\nNo idea how it's possible to do perma cache", "name": "Peter Gostev", "handle": "petergostev", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1934694573797670912/1gnGJwlr_200x200.jpg", "likes": 616, "rts": 1, "replies": 45, "views": 39226, "ts": 1792567763, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112712984644784534", "url": "https://x.com/thsottiaux/status/2112712984644784534", "text": "Day 16/\n\nYou can now cache for 12h in the API and for logged in users across Codex & Work we keep cache forever and you don’t have any penalty for coming back to an old conversation or resuming after a few hours and are now guaranteed to hit the cache.", "name": "Tibo", "handle": "thsottiaux", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2093807917833281537/2yBgpwVV_200x200.jpg", "likes": 15189, "rts": 677, "replies": 2560, "views": 2659999, "ts": 1792545002, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.09, "thread": [], "tier": "must"}, {"id": "2112923503100125326", "url": "https://x.com/cohere/status/2112923503100125326", "text": "Introducing Rerank 5\n\nA few lines of code can change how you search. With this release, we’re advancing the frontier in AI retrieval and precision.\n\nAvailable in Pro and Fast tiers today.", "name": "Cohere", "handle": "cohere", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1650250832909152260/760DZ0cv_200x200.png", "likes": 341, "rts": 38, "replies": 20, "views": 24319, "ts": 1792595194, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKZyeAXEAArPk_.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.09, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112962250537369654", "url": "https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112962250537369654", "text": "When a fix ships, Claude checks the changed code and marks the finding fixed, even if your team fixed it outside Claude Security. If the issue comes back, the finding reopens.\n\nEach project also gets a threat model Claude writes from your code, which your team can edit.", "name": "Claude", "handle": "claudeai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1950950107937185792/QOfEjFoJ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 367, "rts": 6, "replies": 22, "views": 74757, "ts": 1792604432, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2112962248218148878", "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.09, "thread": [{"id": "2112962248218148878", "url": "https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112962248218148878", "text": "Claude Security now keeps up with your code as it changes.\n\nConnect your repositories once. Claude runs a deep first scan, then re-scans as changes land, so findings stay close to the current state of your code.\n\nIn public beta on Claude Enterprise.", "name": "Claude", "handle": "claudeai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1950950107937185792/QOfEjFoJ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 8031, "rts": 408, "replies": 307, "views": 495966, "ts": 1792604431, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVK4ttCWMAAxox-.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112979782652813783", "url": "https://x.com/github/status/2112979782652813783", "text": "As traffic grew exponentially, we moved 99.99999% of GitHub's Git repos to Azure. A new write path moves the last, busiest repos, keeps cross-region round trips from capping throughput, and enables stateless Azure Blob Storage replicas. https://x.com/i/article/2112972394310885376", "name": "GitHub", "handle": "github", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2051404708766507008/ATxxTJXO_200x200.jpg", "likes": 249, "rts": 26, "replies": 32, "views": 37874, "ts": 1792608612, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.07, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112907155787411582", "url": "https://x.com/AndrewCurran_/status/2112907155787411582", "text": "GPTs will also now be available in Antigravity.\nhttps://openai.com/index/openai-models-google-cloud/", "name": "Andrew Curran", "handle": "AndrewCurran_", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1596945208058744833/_X3LT7fb_200x200.jpg", "likes": 362, "rts": 20, "replies": 25, "views": 15616, "ts": 1792591296, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.05, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112941801510178862", "url": "https://x.com/elonmusk/status/2112941801510178862", "text": "Send 𝕏 links to your friends so that they know what’s really going on", "name": "Elon Musk", "handle": "elonmusk", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2103567689515954176/aR-Kjpp4_200x200.jpg", "likes": 41556, "rts": 6569, "replies": 2227, "views": 22423064, "ts": 1792599556, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112939813225849085", "url": "https://x.com/cb_doge/status/2112939813225849085", "text": "\"If you talk to somebody who gets all their information from legacy media, they are living in a different world than if they are listening to your podcast or getting news from 𝕏. They are living in an alternate reality.\"\n\n— Elon Musk", "name": "DogeDesigner", "handle": "cb_doge", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1498070100393754625/C2V-fbll_200x200.jpg", "likes": 5616, "rts": 1338, "replies": 384, "views": 22246629, "ts": 1792599082, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2002631782278311948/vid/avc1/540x540/37KeY1G1uYCL-8Fo.mp4?tag=21", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2002631782278311948/vid/avc1/720x720/Wo2bzQ5kzAxZngoM.mp4?tag=21", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2002631782278311948/img/dWsJvCjwouOTLNh5.jpg", "dur": 65}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Everything Else", "score": 6.04, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112955412752568714", "url": "https://x.com/aisearchio/status/2112955412752568714", "text": "Clever hack to use MOSS with reference images\n\nhttps://github.com/ThetaCursed/ComfyUI-MOSSImageTextEncoder", "name": "AI Search", "handle": "aisearchio", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1766883795150479360/uu_KeDD1_200x200.jpg", "likes": 328, "rts": 19, "replies": 2, "views": 14657, "ts": 1792602801, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVK5l42boAASLuD.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 6.03, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112984783068561883", "url": "https://x.com/LuminaBench/status/2112984783068561883", "text": "Anthropic are launching Claude Duet\n\nThis looks like their version of Grok bot / OpenAI's dots from what I can see", "name": "Lumina", "handle": "LuminaBench", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2062955151820787712/m39pUS7O_200x200.jpg", "likes": 292, "rts": 5, "replies": 18, "views": 10078, "ts": 1792609804, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLTWNyW8AIIsPb.png?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.02, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112941753510895739", "url": "https://x.com/eriktorenberg/status/2112941753510895739", "text": "Excited to announce the Cosign Awards: celebrating exceptional people, companies, and products that had a massive impact in 2026.\n\nhttp://www.cosign.co/awards\n\nAnyone can nominate and vote. Award winners will be chosen by the public in conjunction with a panel of judges, including industry leaders like @lennysan, @packyM, @amasad, and many more. We’ll host an event in December honoring the winners (10 per category).\n\nFor people categories, we’re mostly focused on everyone except the CEO–everyone else building the company behind the scenes. \n\nCompanies with the most amount of people nominations will be listed on our “Top Companies to Work For” page. \n\nTrue to form, at Product Hunt I helped launch the Golden Kitty awards a decade ago to recognize great products. Soon after I launched the Rise Awards, honoring executives and ICs building companies that make them. The Cosign Awards is the next iteration. \n\nWe have 41 categories across people, companies, and products.\n\nPeople categories:\n- Future Founders\n- Rising Stars\n- Engineering\n- FDE\n- Product\n- Design\n- AI Researchers\n- Marketing\n- Comms\n- Growth\n- Chief of Staff\n- Operations\n- Talent\n- Finance\n- Public Intellectuals\n- Podcaster of the Year\n- Newsletter Author of the Year\n- Poster of the Year\n\nCompany categories:\n- Rising Stars\n- Best Place to Work\n- Founder Mafia\n- Talent Density\n- Highest Aura\n\nProduct categories:\n- Product of the Year\n- Launch of the Year\n- AI Assistant\n- AI Language Model\n- AI Coding\n- Dev Tools\n- Open Source\n- Consumer\n- Productivity\n- Creative & Media Tools\n- Product Design & UX\n- Enterprise B2B\n- Hardware\n- Robotics\n- Self-Driving\n- Healthcare\n- Vertical Fintech\n- Legal", "name": "Erik Torenberg", "handle": "eriktorenberg", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1449117890955788292/x3DIqECG_200x200.jpg", "likes": 220, "rts": 30, "replies": 36, "views": 64627, "ts": 1792599545, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKskW6bQAE2vUu.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 6.01, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112801629720236509", "url": "https://x.com/ChrisGPT/status/2112801629720236509", "text": "Dario finally confirms Anthropic is:\n\n“\"Anthropic is over 5,000 people.\" They’re catching up to OAI.\n\nsome other quotes I enjoyed.\n\n“We likely sacrificed a significant chunk of a several-month model lead.” - speaking about mythos.\n\n\"I will have more to say soon about how we are going to further live our values in this moment of rapid change.\"\n\n\"We are nearing that point and that’s what’s driving some of our thinking about 'pacing.'\"", "name": "Chris", "handle": "ChrisGPT", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2046736394152812544/LfmDgg0I_200x200.jpg", "likes": 493, "rts": 14, "replies": 20, "views": 66131, "ts": 1792566137, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112732230124818891", "url": "https://x.com/DarioAmodei/status/2112732230124818891", "text": "Thank you for this, @_NathanCalvin. I appreciate and am a fan of the investigative work you’ve done on behalf of AI safety and believe your comments are coming from a constructive, honest place, and that it benefits the public to have people whose job is to scrutinize the actions of frontier AI companies. I think something would be wrong if companies of the size of Anthropic didn’t get harshly criticized basically every day; strong antibodies against corporate power are a healthy feature of a free society, and especially so when issues of the gravity of the one we’re facing are at play. I also do agree that some of the examples you cite were mistakes by Anthropic.\n\nThat said, I disagree with the thrust of your post. At the core, a good fraction of the things you criticize are consequences of Anthropic’s basic theory of change that we have held for many years. Our strategy has always been what I’ve called the “race to the top” — by showing how it’s possible to build the technology responsibly, we can raise the bar for the entire industry. The forces that drive the race to the top are hugely more effective if the company practicing them is at the frontier; that’s one lesson I’ve learned repeatedly. And looking at that strategy as a whole, I’m proud of our track record and stand by it.\n\nI believe that if we take a list of things that Anthropic did in the past year that were pro-safety and commercially costly to us, they far outweigh the list you’ve made, while at the same time I also disagree with some of the items on your list. Here are some things Anthropic has done, in the same period you’ve cited (the last year), which were very costly to us commercially while having strong safety benefits:\n\n1. We played a key role in SB53 (and other state laws), at a time when doing so was politically perilous to the company and AI regulation was very unpopular. We in fact argued for a stronger version of SB53 than was ultimately passed; there were a lot of stakeholders and we were not the limiting factor in the strength of the bill that was passed.\n\n2. We have very strong biological safeguards on our models, because we’ve seen concerning results in bio uplift trials and even seen and blocked potential attempts to misuse our models for bioweapons (as seen in our threat intelligence report from a few weeks ago). This is a very serious threat that we want to have zero tolerance for. But having very low false negatives means having significant false positives (despite months of effort on our part to improve the classifiers), enough so that biologists joke about how restrictive our classifiers are, and competitors sometimes sell against us by touting their more permissive bio capabilities. This has been very painful to us in an area where we care a lot about supporting an ecosystem of Claude users (biomedical science; and it is particularly frustrating to me as a biologist), but it’s the right thing to do and we intend to keep doing it.\n\n3. Similarly, we first had Mythos-class models in February but didn’t make them generally available until June (when we had stronger cyber safeguards in place). We only offered them to a limited set of partners in order to focus on cyberdefense. It would have been much more economically rational to simply release the model to everyone. We likely sacrificed a significant chunk of a several-month model lead. But because of this, many companies secured their cyber infrastructure before the deluge of widely available cyber-capable models. Of course there are ways it could have gone better or been run better, but this was what we were aiming to do.\n\n4. I advocated forcefully and publicly, in my essay Policy on the AI Exponential in June of this year, for the government to have the power to block model releases. Back then this was still outside or maybe on the edge of the Overton window, though I understand the window has moved since then. We were attacked intensely for this.\n\n5. I don’t want to re-ignite conflict, but the events of February 2026 are a strong example. It is clear that our public stance could have seriously damaged the company’s future.\n\n6. We’ve continued to advocate for export controls on chips and chipmaking equipment to China, because we think it allows democracies to negotiate from a position of strength on AI safety agreements. We’ve done this despite problems it’s created for us with industry partners who hold very different views and on whom we are very dependent.\n\n7. As far as I’m aware we were the first, and still only, frontier company to restrict the sale of AI services to PRC-controlled companies, including those operating through third countries (otherwise a major loophole). We’ve therefore forfeited significant short-term business to prevent fueling AI platforms and applications that would help the CCP’s military and intelligence services. We are also cracking down on distillation; generally, distillation is not a major threat to us commercially, but like chip exports it has concerning national security and safety implications, so we expend significant energy and company resources to address it.\n\n8. As you mentioned, we’ve put out a lot of reports, announcements, long model cards, public advocacy, etc, all trying to inform the public about the risks of AI. These involve a level of detail and effort that would be implausible for a purely profit-maximizing company.\n\n9. Just this week, we announced we were putting 5% (and possibly more over time) of the company into a public benefit fund.\n\nAll of these decisions came with very substantial costs to us. To be fair, those that involved holding back or restricting access to frontier capabilities also had a broader cost — for example, the public might otherwise have benefitted from faster progress in biology, so I am not saying that our decisions were without tradeoffs — but they were not driven by profit-seeking.\n\nFor each decision, you can come up with ulterior motives that could have driven us — I think it is a general truth that any decision, on any topic, can always be portrayed as a nefarious scheme. But most of those end up being pretty farfetched or don’t fully make sense if you actually examine them closely. For example, it is deeply implausible that it would be a shrewd business move to block a large swath of research biology in order to do the work ourselves and compete with our customers; we expect the benefit to the ecosystem to greatly exceed the work Anthropic does itself, and for the two to complement each other. It is not in Anthropic’s interest to be perceived as unfriendly to biologists. Our Life Sciences Verification Program, which gives vetted biology professionals access to our models, also refutes this idea. \n\nOf course, a different set of people will see this and make a different criticism: that Anthropic shouldn't be making decisions about who gets access to what in the first place. I agree, in the sense that ideally the government would be making these decisions, and that’s why we advocate for regulation. But for now we are forced to make them, and enabling bioterrorism isn’t a better option.\n\nI think that even if I conceded every item in your list, I would happily hold up mine next to it; I don’t think it’s a close call. That is of course partly subjective and different things are important to different people. Maybe one way you could object is that several of the items above relate to AI misuse and not loss-of-control; if you think the latter is much more important than the former, that might change your calculus. But this is not Anthropic’s view — we view both as serious issues. You can also disagree with our decisions, as many have, but they are consistent.\n\nBut I also disagree with at least some of your list. Several items don’t represent a deviation from Anthropic’s strategy or values at all, once we postulate the “race to the top” strategy that requires being a frontier AI company. Your items 1-4 are all simply examples of what it means to compete at the frontier, and we stand by them, both in the sense that they are transparently ordinary things to do as part of that strategy, and that we stand by the strategy as a whole. \n\nTo be fair, there are important questions worth confronting (and that we are confronting) about what to do when the AI-driven acceleration gets fast enough that it is hard for safety to keep up. We are nearing that point and that’s what’s driving some of our thinking about “pacing.” But 1-4 are actions taken before that point and should be perfectly expected based on Anthropic’s strategy as a company. Of course it is possible to disagree with that strategy, but that’s a more fundamental disagreement that you could have had any time over the last few years.\n\nI agree we could have handled your items 7, 8, and 9 better. All fall into the category of “rhetoric or policy communication that wasn’t carefully written or resulting from fast-changing circumstances not propagating from one team to another fast enough.” This kind of thing happens a lot in large organizations (Anthropic is over 5,000 people), and doesn’t even necessarily represent a failure or mistake of any single person. I am of course the CEO, and I take responsibility. The volume of communication from Anthropic is enormous and if these are the worst examples, I think we’re frankly doing pretty well, though I will continue to work hard to make sure we do even better.\n\nYour 5th item (the change to our RSP) is the most substantive item, and people can debate this, but I think that the old, more rigid RSP created a lot of situations where we were technically required to do something that required a lot of effort but in retrospect wasn’t very important for safety, as well as not covering some of the most important things. Our current RSP’s focus on transparency brought things like risk reports and roadmaps, and its emphasis on independent external review laid the groundwork for even more intensive embedded evaluation, all of which I think have been valuable in driving a race to the top. In the original announcement in September 2023, we described the RSP as an “early iteration that we [would] build on” and said that “rapid iteration…will almost certainly be necessary.” I understand that changing plans is always a move that creates justifiable scrutiny — I just don’t think we ever represented that we wouldn’t do so.\n\nRelating to your 6th item (having an FCF in addition to our RSP), I know our intent is to meet the letter and spirit of our regulatory obligations (via our FCF) while also remaining able to experiment with more novel approaches to risk management that go beyond what the law currently requires (via our RSP). I promise to look into it further, as it’s a complex issue and it is possible we are not weighing the considerations in the right way.\n\nOverall, although Anthropic has made plenty of mistakes as an organization — including some of the items on your list — I feel that we have repeatedly taken serious and substantive actions counter to our bottom line and that there is a significant negativity bias distorting assessments like the one you are making above. We’ll always try to do better but, again, I am extremely proud of our track record and stand by it. I will have more to say soon about how we are going to further live our values in this moment of rapid change.", "name": "Dario Amodei", "handle": "DarioAmodei", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2015835742577012736/uOwdzrEz_200x200.jpg", "likes": 3925, "rts": 302, "replies": 703, "views": 1350149, "ts": 1792549590, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.99, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112997391351873855", "url": "https://x.com/ChrisGPT/status/2112997391351873855", "text": "Two weeks ago, I had shared that Anthropic was working on their Dots competitor.\n\nAnthropic has now unveiled Claude Duet, and it’s arriving much earlier than I expected!\n\nDuet is a proactive personal AI agent designed to work on your behalf around the clock. (Just like dots and grok bot) It connects to your email, calendar, and other applications to monitor important developments, prepare daily briefings, manage tasks, and autonomously work on longer projects.\n\nIt’s currently rolling out in research preview through a waitlist. Really excited to see Anthropic entering the personal agent space hopefully they’re able to fill in the gaps missing from Dots and Grok bot!", "name": "Chris", "handle": "ChrisGPT", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2046736394152812544/LfmDgg0I_200x200.jpg", "likes": 240, "rts": 6, "replies": 25, "views": 15535, "ts": 1792612810, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLf7fDWgAAtQ4W.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": {"id": "2107682555377955009", "url": "https://x.com/ChrisGPT/status/2107682555377955009", "text": "I’m hearing preliminary rumblings of Anthropic prepping their dots / grok bot competitor. Early stages\n\nI could only imagine how many people would jump ship for an Ultra fast fable 5.5 agent.\n\nI am being wishful when I say “ultra fast”\n\nOpenAI is already prepping new fast chips. They can really pull away if they’re able to get out a 6.5 level model at Ultra fast speeds before Fable 5.5.\n\nOpus 5.5 is a phenomenal coding model.", "name": "Chris", "handle": "ChrisGPT", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2046736394152812544/LfmDgg0I_200x200.jpg", "likes": 300, "rts": 5, "replies": 21, "views": 45729, "ts": 1791345654, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.99, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2113012534513188912", "url": "https://x.com/ArtificialAnlys/status/2113012534513188912", "text": "In AA-PersonalityBench, Gemini models are the most Confident and the least likely to Admit Uncertainty of all models measured\n\n➤ Most Confident: Gemini 3.8 Flash (100), Gemini 4 Argon (97) and Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview (95)\n➤ Least likely to Admit Uncertainty: Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview (1), Gemini 4 Argon (4) and Gemini 3.8 Flash (6)", "name": "Artificial Analysis", "handle": "ArtificialAnlys", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2042402069320290304/A8C1lP07_200x200.jpg", "likes": 229, "rts": 11, "replies": 13, "views": 20007, "ts": 1792616420, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLtkxlWEAA2HiD.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.98, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112822997161562600", "url": "https://x.com/LuminaBench/status/2112822997161562600", "text": "Gemini 4 Carbon Voxel Pagoda\n\nCarbon has been in arena since yesterday, so I will show off some outputs I got over today\n\nIt's a noticeable improvement over Argon but is incredibly slow, like 2-4x slower depending on task", "name": "Lumina", "handle": "LuminaBench", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2062955151820787712/m39pUS7O_200x200.jpg", "likes": 417, "rts": 12, "replies": 30, "views": 31989, "ts": 1792571231, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112821820793434112/vid/avc1/1056x720/Ux6ThNgMAHmZYu2G.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112821820793434112/vid/avc1/1880x1280/MQLValHS8JTfQGkj.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112821820793434112/img/WH5Vxe9DB5y5S9hk.jpg", "dur": 27}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.97, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112992019182420013", "url": "https://x.com/joypbuilds/status/2112992019182420013", "text": "time to make an actual intro post for nyc since last time was a shitpost\n\nhi i’m joy and i:\n- am a founder building stitch, a mini vlog app with 1-2k organic dau (http://www.timetoviva.com)\n- flew in to sf for @fdotinc in may\n- growth lead at @pally as party master\n- landed in nyc a few days ago to explore if this is the city to build taste based consumer app\n- am trying to host a halloween pregaming party\n\ni want to know:\n- who are the most cracked consumer founders & designers in nyc i should hit up?\n- who do i need to connect with to throw the sickest events?\n- will you be at my party\n\nplz twitter help me find cool ppl thanks", "name": "joy p", "handle": "joypbuilds", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2028336851392872450/h3wFMyqG_200x200.jpg", "likes": 206, "rts": 6, "replies": 38, "views": 7947, "ts": 1792611529, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLbCsbXMAEjoFp.jpg?name=orig"}, {"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLbCsQW4AAcr4a.jpg?name=orig"}, {"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLbCsqWoAAWijq.jpg?name=orig"}, {"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVLbCsLXgAANlR0.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.97, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112890640145252396", "url": "https://x.com/ai_for_success/status/2112890640145252396", "text": "🚨 OpenAI is bringing its frontier models and Codex agentic workflows to Google Cloud’s Model Garden.\n\nGoogle keeps winning, one way or another.", "name": "AshutoshShrivastava", "handle": "ai_for_success", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1687401026805256192/uQ1o2wVQ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 316, "rts": 7, "replies": 30, "views": 13293, "ts": 1792587358, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVJ-RY6aUAAWKTr.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.95, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112962252706124134", "url": "https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112962252706124134", "text": "Projects can span repositories from GitHub, GitLab, and other Git providers, and findings reach your tools through a new API and MCP connector.\n\nEligible orgs receive $25K in usage credits each month, for two months, through Dec 31, 2026. Terms apply: https://claude.com/resources/articles/claude-security-now-scans-continuously-and-tracks-fixes", "name": "Claude", "handle": "claudeai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1950950107937185792/QOfEjFoJ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 262, "rts": 4, "replies": 8, "views": 50067, "ts": 1792604432, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2112962250537369654", "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.92, "thread": [{"id": "2112962248218148878", "url": "https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112962248218148878", "text": "Claude Security now keeps up with your code as it changes.\n\nConnect your repositories once. Claude runs a deep first scan, then re-scans as changes land, so findings stay close to the current state of your code.\n\nIn public beta on Claude Enterprise.", "name": "Claude", "handle": "claudeai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1950950107937185792/QOfEjFoJ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 8031, "rts": 408, "replies": 307, "views": 495966, "ts": 1792604431, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVK4ttCWMAAxox-.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, {"id": "2112962250537369654", "url": "https://x.com/claudeai/status/2112962250537369654", "text": "When a fix ships, Claude checks the changed code and marks the finding fixed, even if your team fixed it outside Claude Security. If the issue comes back, the finding reopens.\n\nEach project also gets a threat model Claude writes from your code, which your team can edit.", "name": "Claude", "handle": "claudeai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1950950107937185792/QOfEjFoJ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 367, "rts": 6, "replies": 22, "views": 74757, "ts": 1792604432, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2112962248218148878"}], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112909535161577489", "url": "https://x.com/typecraft_dev/status/2112909535161577489", "text": "This is absolutely hilarious, and I love it! I wonder if in practice this really works well. Maybe I should test in stream? https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail", "name": "typecraft", "handle": "typecraft_dev", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1903838655556558848/44VeJb6q_200x200.jpg", "likes": 239, "rts": 5, "replies": 43, "views": 30815, "ts": 1792591863, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.91, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112861576952676404", "url": "https://x.com/LuminaBench/status/2112861576952676404", "text": "🚨 Kimi K3.1 is now getting prepped in Kimi Code\n\nThere is a model switching warning that now includes Kimi K3.1 if you change model mid session\n\nNo exact time of launch but it won't be far away now", "name": "Lumina", "handle": "LuminaBench", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2062955151820787712/m39pUS7O_200x200.jpg", "likes": 305, "rts": 16, "replies": 15, "views": 22969, "ts": 1792580429, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVJivskW4AI8ejx.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.9, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112935427422527584", "url": "https://x.com/ArtificialAnlys/status/2112935427422527584", "text": "We've updated MLCR-AA, our leaderboard for Wisedocs' Medical Long Context Reasoning benchmark, with Claude Sonnet 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra now tied for the #1 score\n\nWe've updated how MLCR-AA prompts and grades models on @wisedocsai's benchmark, to better reflect how medical record review is done in practice. Models are now prompted as medical record reviewers, and asked to answer each part of a question in one paragraph with their conclusion and the supporting evidence from the record. Answers are graded by a new three-model judge panel, and we reviewed all 60 expert reference answers against their source records, refining 15.\n\nClaude Sonnet 5.5 (max with fallback) from @AnthropicAI and GPT-6 Astra (max) from @OpenAI are tied at the top with 41.7%, followed by GPT-6.1 Sol (max) at 38.3%. Qwen3.8 2.4T A95B from @Alibaba_Qwen is the leading open weights model at 30.6%.\n\nKey results from the updated MLCR-AA:\n\n➤ Medical record review remains far from solved: an answer passes only when it is both complete and accurate, and no model passes more than 42% of questions and the median model passes under 15%\n\n➤ Anthropic models lead on completeness, reaching the expert's conclusion with the right medical reasoning: Claude models hold the top 18 completeness scores across every model and effort level we tested, led by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (max with fallback) at 64.8%. The best non-Anthropic model is GPT-6 Astra (max) at 42.2%\n\n➤ OpenAI models lead on accuracy, citing the record without exaggeration or hallucination: OpenAI models hold the top 27 accuracy scores, led by GPT-6.1 Sol (max) at 96.7%, but answer fewer questions completely: GPT-6.1 Sol (max) is complete on 40.0% of questions\n\nWe would like to thank Wisedocs for building MLCR and for their collaboration in bringing it to Artificial Analysis", "name": "Artificial Analysis", "handle": "ArtificialAnlys", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2042402069320290304/A8C1lP07_200x200.jpg", "likes": 195, "rts": 16, "replies": 29, "views": 29934, "ts": 1792598036, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKmYIbb0AAuF42.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.9, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112927257794887689", "url": "https://x.com/lyraxana/status/2112927257794887689", "text": "[Kimi K3.1] Ana de Armas SVG\n~49k tokens; ~22 minutes; effort: max; zero-shot", "name": "lyra", "handle": "lyraxana", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2093829072925192192/BBcihBo3_200x200.jpg", "likes": 216, "rts": 0, "replies": 47, "views": 32402, "ts": 1792596089, "media": [{"type": "photo", "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVKgIYiXIAAyKm-.jpg?name=orig"}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.9, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}]);